#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175623 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92d6129b-a5c5-597a-86ee-3f19af20f326" :knowledge-point/title "Describing the General Properties of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/id #uuid "12dc1c50-69bc-54d8-8d60-a7fc144c9644" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-11008" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the graph of $y=\\csc x$?\n\n1. The function takes the value $1$ at $x = -\\dfrac {\\pi} 2 + 2 \\pi n$, where $n$ is an integer\n2. The range of the function is $(-\\infty,+\\infty)$\n3. The function has zeroes at $x = 0, \\pm \\pi, \\pm 2 \\pi,\\dots$" :question/worked-solution "Let's have a look at the graph of $y=\\csc x$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3567/Source/Images/203bd5e873deaa54322cd7fb4cb6610d.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The cosecant takes the value $1$ at $x = \\dfrac {\\pi} 2 + 2 \\pi n$. Therefore, statement I is false.\n- The range of $y = \\csc x$ is $[MATH: (-\\infty,-1] \\cup [1, \\infty).]$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n- The cosecant graph does not intersect the $x$-axis. Therefore, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175624 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37434c3e-2269-5b08-94c5-7516df7879b2" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/id #uuid "a1713375-241f-5a53-a109-3c3d0f917411" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-11009" :question/problem "If $\\csc x = 8$, then what is $\\csc(x-8\\pi)$?" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y = \\csc x$ is $2\\pi$. Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = \\csc{(x+2n\\pi)}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-4$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\csc x &= \\csc (x + 2 \\cdot (-4) \\cdot π) \\\\\n&= \\csc (x - 8π)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = 8\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\csc{(x-8\\pi)} = 8\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175630 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a710f9-fed1-5fdb-b4c0-fd3ae2d4d8a4" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/id #uuid "12eb5678-b62b-50ad-97bf-88768c4bf365" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-11015" :question/problem "If $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{7}{2}$, then find the value of $\\csc(-x - 20\\pi)$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\csc{x}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3567/Source/Images/8c39f3895fd26e9d53a573809cdb9ee3.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\csc{x}$ is $2\\pi$.\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\csc{x}$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n$\\csc(-x) = -\\csc{x}$.\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\csc{x}$, we have\n$\\csc (-x - 20π)|= \\csc (-x - 2π \\cdot 10); = \\csc (-x)$.\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\csc{x}$, we have\n$\\csc (-x)|=-\\csc x; =-\\frac{7}{2}$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\csc(-x - 20\\pi) = -\\dfrac{7}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92d6129b-a5c5-597a-86ee-3f19af20f326" :knowledge-point/title "Describing the General Properties of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e61d67e0-b2ea-5d42-9c5b-d5dd15090e74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248400} {:answer/id #uuid "64f5a113-3a93-5f49-a906-264d8e2e4129" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248401} {:answer/id #uuid "bf58768a-7a58-5816-87b4-b3839e2d1327" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248402} {:answer/id #uuid "e25101ff-788d-5793-b8a3-c964b279cd69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248403} {:answer/id #uuid "2a26a132-1541-51f6-bfeb-c1e7fd574f70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e25101ff-788d-5793-b8a3-c964b279cd69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248403} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa80557c-13ec-5d35-a6ac-fe232a00e958" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248399}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d63e671d-d666-591d-bcdc-e6dce8b04cf5" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-103811" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x?$\n\n- The domain of the function is all positive real numbers.\n- $x=\\frac{9\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote of the function.\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-4\\pi )=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+6\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13940224/assets/f1b80c29b8f9f4d33aae94fc287d95b97cc5865b36ecc98f4f41e625dfc842e1.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The function's domain is all real numbers except the integer multiples of $\\pi $, such as $0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,….$ Hence, the statement I is false.\n\n- The lines $x=n\\pi $ for any integer $n,$ are the vertical asymptotes of the function. Hence, statement II is false.\n\n- The period of the function is $2\\pi .$ So, $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-4\\pi )=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+6\\pi ).$ Hence, statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248405 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92d6129b-a5c5-597a-86ee-3f19af20f326" :knowledge-point/title "Describing the General Properties of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6a2e36f-0239-5df2-b7b3-1d59f05b058b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248407} {:answer/id #uuid "ab80896a-b944-58cf-b0be-edd29a582fe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248408} {:answer/id #uuid "ad21e286-94d3-5a3e-a2b6-5be8c1900ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248409} {:answer/id #uuid "4b1d234b-d186-5de3-97be-9401dcb66c2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248410} {:answer/id #uuid "f8872c4c-2a0b-5cec-beed-452193a55403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6a2e36f-0239-5df2-b7b3-1d59f05b058b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248407} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0dd5e76-906c-52c4-bfcd-fc4b42c696f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248406}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4383ecaa-1c57-5705-8853-cba2341fc9fa" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-103812" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x?$\n\n- The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1].$\n- $y=-1$ is the maximum value of the function.\n- $x=15\\pi $ is in the domain." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13940224/assets/f1b80c29b8f9f4d33aae94fc287d95b97cc5865b36ecc98f4f41e625dfc842e1.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ therefore the function doesn't have a maximum value. Hence, statements I and II are both false.\n\n- The function's domain is all real numbers except the integer multiples of $\\pi $, such as $0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,….$ Hence, statement III is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92d6129b-a5c5-597a-86ee-3f19af20f326" :knowledge-point/title "Describing the General Properties of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6169b1f9-2f5c-5472-95da-ac7ac922ae81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248414} {:answer/id #uuid "98752cd8-424d-5e02-b4ce-0fa64f274c9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248415} {:answer/id #uuid "da212c45-9a31-5a10-ae17-4cbcf0113812" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248416} {:answer/id #uuid "936af1ed-f3e2-5e3a-9f44-2e9fabb8dff7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248417} {:answer/id #uuid "30804f63-c71e-5af3-a2d8-4ca485011915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98752cd8-424d-5e02-b4ce-0fa64f274c9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248415} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b490ebb-4961-552f-8f50-6a4a8eb39305" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248413}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5aee14ce-41d0-51b6-96b0-a07b706c537b" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-103814" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x?$ Assume $n$ is an integer.\n\n- $y=1$ is the minimum value of the function. $$\n- $x=9\\pi $ is a vertical asymptote of the function.\n- The function takes the value $1$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13940224/assets/066fe6ce8a65927436d64753ec4e61bfea257405e4b2f81554ac8341572bcf15.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )$, therefore the function doesn't have a minimum value. Hence, statement I is false.\n\n- The lines $x=n\\pi $ for any integer $n$ are the vertical asymptotes of the function. Hence statement II is true.\n\n- The cosecant takes the value $1$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi .$ Hence statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37434c3e-2269-5b08-94c5-7516df7879b2" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0869d50-4293-55a0-a292-5c482622b1d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248421} {:answer/id #uuid "0c5947d6-2268-5762-96eb-882811f7b8f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(\\pi +6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248422} {:answer/id #uuid "fef29507-fe7d-5db7-b342-785fffc1e9e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248423} {:answer/id #uuid "41751aae-b782-5d8c-84f8-5624b29c2f41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248424} {:answer/id #uuid "2340d93f-d59d-5efc-ab02-e6dda4bc0154" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0869d50-4293-55a0-a292-5c482622b1d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248421} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaa2ecf2-b6c1-5e6f-9b04-b0f9d2bc556d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248420}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eaa7b309-c8e9-5ca2-a11e-2cdf142dad7c" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-103818" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=3,$ then what is $5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+12\\pi )?$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=6$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2\\cdot 6\\cdot \\pi )=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+12\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=3,$ then $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+12\\pi )=3.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+12\\pi )=5\\cdot 3=15.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37434c3e-2269-5b08-94c5-7516df7879b2" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ac5c6a4-7044-5b3e-9710-7cdd04a62bfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248428} {:answer/id #uuid "a40a5e84-be3a-50ca-9292-61d2d56d6a5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248429} {:answer/id #uuid "0c78fd9e-315e-55e5-b275-1d1f197f5b0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248430} {:answer/id #uuid "0fba7643-1aae-5427-a129-a245f8738fed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248431} {:answer/id #uuid "d344d922-4f7c-550c-8223-1b55704399a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ac5c6a4-7044-5b3e-9710-7cdd04a62bfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248428} :answer-field/id #uuid "e11a372e-3b53-5d20-9689-2a799bb7e694" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248427}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "897c4e4e-8426-5730-9251-80bbb10789f6" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-103820" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\sqrt{5},$ then what is $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+14\\pi )?$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=7$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2\\cdot 7\\cdot \\pi )=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+14\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\sqrt{5},$ then $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+14\\pi )=\\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37434c3e-2269-5b08-94c5-7516df7879b2" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4985d07-3eef-56a8-89dd-d1711128ad59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248435} {:answer/id #uuid "55a52eb5-915c-571c-afd6-5b0f427e7014" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248436} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc5dec9-7f64-5b90-b71b-d6326f72de6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248437} {:answer/id #uuid "e2760f35-3658-5196-9bcc-68d3af32df16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248438} {:answer/id #uuid "58172275-a52c-52ad-a3a0-5afd5bf69abb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcc5dec9-7f64-5b90-b71b-d6326f72de6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248437} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3887d24-25ba-522a-8729-2c7bf051d914" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248434}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "172ea96d-b239-53d5-9f76-8cc24f2c7e3f" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-103822" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=6,$ then what is $\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+16\\pi )+5?$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=8$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2\\cdot 8\\cdot \\pi )=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+16\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=6,$ then $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+16\\pi )=6.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+16\\pi )+5 & =\\frac{1}{3}(6)+5 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3}+5 \\\\  & =2+5 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a710f9-fed1-5fdb-b4c0-fd3ae2d4d8a4" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answe